      "text": "(~200 ppm) by foam fractionation. Since groundwater and surface water has a wide range of cationic species, there is a need for a study to investigate the effect of various cations/salts on the removal of PFAS via foam fractionation, particularly at low PFAS concentrations which are encountered often in commercial applications. This paper investigates the effect of different cations on the removal of a variety of PFAS with different chain length and functional groups by foam fractionation. The tests were conducted using simulated as well as real groundwater samples. A hydrocarbon surfactant (SDS) was used as a co-foaming agent to assist with foam generation as the PFAS concentrations used here (and generally as present in waste and surface waters) are not high enough to cause foaming by themselves ( < 50 ppb). This study also includes the removal of long and short-chain PFAS in sulphonic and carboxylic acid forms. 2. Materials and method 2.1. Chemicals PFHxS ( > 97% purity) was purchased from BOC Sciences. PFOS ( > 40% purity), PFOA ( > 95% purity), and PFBA ( > 97% purity) in acid form were purchased from Merck. The chemical structure for these different PFAS are summarised below in Table 1 . Sodium dodecyl sulphate (SDS), sodium chloride (NaCl), magnesium"
